{"name":"Sunflower Mango","hex":"#ffb700","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sunflower","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any plant of the genus Helianthus, so called probably from the form and color of its floral head, having the form of a large disk surrounded by yellow ray flowers.\n\nThe commonly cultivated species, Helianthus annuus, a native of America.","The commonly cultivated species, Helianthus annuus, a native of America."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sunflower"},{"term":"mango","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of species Mangifera indica of tropical Asian fruit trees.","Fruit of the mango tree."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To stuff and pickle (a fruit)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mango"}],"entries":[{"title":"Common sunflower","blocks":[{"text":"The common sunflower is a large annual forb in the daisy family Asteraceae. The domesticated form of common sunflower is harvested for its edible seeds, which come in two types: oil and confectionery seeds. Oilseed sunflowers are widely grown globally and represent the fourth most used vegetable oil in the world. They also are used widely as bird food or as food for livestock. In contrast, confectionery sunflower seeds are often eaten as a snack food or in baking. There also are horticultural sunflower varieties that are used as plantings in domestic gardens for aesthetics. Wild plants are known for their multiple flower heads, whereas the domestic sunflower often possesses a single large flower head atop an unbranched stem."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Sunflower_sky_backdrop.jpg/330px-Sunflower_sky_backdrop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_sunflower"},{"title":"Mango","blocks":[{"text":"A mango is an edible stone fruit produced by the tropical tree Mangifera indica. It originated in the northeastern part of the South Asia, in what is now Bangladesh, northeastern India and Myanmar. M. indica has been cultivated in South and Southeast Asia since ancient times, resulting in two modern mango cultivar lineages: the \"Indian\" and the \"Southeast Asian\" types. Other species in the genus Mangifera also produce edible fruits called \"mangoes\", most of which are found in the Malesian ecoregion."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Mangos_-_single_and_halved.jpg/330px-Mangos_-_single_and_halved.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mango"},{"title":"Shades of yellow","blocks":[{"text":"Varieties of the color yellow may differ in hue, chroma or lightness, or in two or three of these qualities. Variations in value are also called tints and shades, a tint being a yellow or other hue mixed with white, a shade being mixed with black. A large selection of these various colors is shown below."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Color_icon_yellow.svg/330px-Color_icon_yellow.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_yellow"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/SunflowerSunset2.jpg/500px-SunflowerSunset2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/SunflowerSunset2.jpg/1280px-SunflowerSunset2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/SunflowerSunset2.jpg/500px-SunflowerSunset2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Flower heads facing east, away from the late afternoon sun","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASunflowerSunset2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikip